From 0e3acb6ab4c5e26ab9a8f8fc30de58684d74ebc3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 10:05:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/22] libitm: no SSE barriers for KNC

libitm's x86 port builds x86_sse.cc with -msse, which on k1om gives SSE
moves the k1om assembler rejects.  KNC has no SSE or MMX, so the
compiler never emits calls to the M64/M128 barriers there; compile the
file empty for KNC.  The AVX barriers already cope: without an AVX
assembler they are emitted as raw bytes and only reached on AVX
hardware.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
---
 libitm/config/x86/x86_sse.cc | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libitm/config/x86/x86_sse.cc b/libitm/config/x86/x86_sse.cc
index 5bd50e238c7..a13b54dfffb 100644
--- a/libitm/config/x86/x86_sse.cc
+++ b/libitm/config/x86/x86_sse.cc
@@ -25,6 +25,10 @@
 #include "libitm_i.h"
 #include "dispatch.h"
 
+// KNC has no SSE or MMX: nothing uses these barriers there, and the
+// k1om assembler rejects the SSE moves -msse makes of them.
+#ifndef __KNC__
+
 // ??? Use memcpy for now, until we have figured out how to best instantiate
 // these loads/stores.
 CREATE_DISPATCH_FUNCTIONS_T_MEMCPY(M64, GTM::abi_disp()->, )
@@ -41,3 +45,5 @@ _ITM_LM128 (const _ITM_TYPE_M128 *ptr)
 {
   GTM::GTM_LB (ptr, sizeof (*ptr));
 }
+
+#endif // __KNC__
